---
import Presentation from "../../../../components/Presentation.astro";
import Divider from "../../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";

//#region CodeSnippets
const TwoSources = `COMPUTING INNOVATIONS CAN REFLECT EXISTING
HUMAN BIASES BECAUSE OF

  biases written into the ALGORITHMS

    or

  biases in the DATA used by the innovation

Two sources. Session 44 taught one of them.`;

const AllLevels = `"BIASES CAN BE EMBEDDED AT ALL LEVELS OF
 SOFTWARE DEVELOPMENT."

  what problem you chose to solve
  who you asked about it
  which data you could get
  which variables you kept
  where you set a threshold
  who tested it
  who it shipped to

Every one of those is a decision.`;

const Salary = `let priorSalary = [52000, 41000, 55000, 38000];
let yearsExperience = [5, 5, 6, 6];

function offerFor(i) {
  let offer = priorSalary[i] + (priorSalary[i] / 10);

  if (yearsExperience[i] >= 6) {
    offer = offer + 2000;
  }

  return offer;
}`;

const Amplify = `TWO CANDIDATES, IDENTICAL EXPERIENCE

  prior salary    52,000        41,000
  gap                    11,000

  offer           57,200        45,100
  gap                    12,100

The algorithm did not merely pass the gap along.
It made it BIGGER.`;

const Intent = `"Algorithms can be intentionally or
 unintentionally biased."

Nobody in this example wanted this outcome.
The program has no opinion about anybody.

It is still doing harm, and somebody chose
the variable.`;

const Appropriate = `THE SAME VARIABLE, TWO VERDICTS

  AGE in a life insurance premium ...... appropriate
  AGE in a hiring decision ............. not

  POSTCODE in a delivery estimate ...... appropriate
  POSTCODE in a loan decision .......... not

"Is this variable biased?" is the wrong question.
"Biased FOR WHAT USE?" is the right one.`;

const Collab = `FROM SESSION 53

  "Collaboration that includes diverse perspectives
   helps avoid bias in the development of
   computing innovations."

The people in the room are part of the algorithm.`;
//#endregion
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a href="/courses/ap-csp/" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3">
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Activity 3.13: Computing Bias</h1>
        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />

        <!-- Brief -->
        <section id="brief">
          <h2>You Have Had Half of This Already</h2>

          <p>
            <b>Session 44 taught that bias comes from the data</b> &mdash; from the type
            or source of what was collected, and that collecting more of it does not
            help.
          </p>

          <p>
            <b class="text-sn-theme">The exam names two sources, and the other one is
            the algorithm itself.</b> Today is that half: a program with no bug in it,
            doing harm, because of a variable somebody chose to include.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Launch / Concept -->
        <section id="launch">
          <Presentation>
            <section>
              <h2>Activity 3.13</h2>
              <h3 class="text-sn-theme">Computing Bias</h3>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Key Concepts</h2>
              <p>Two Sources: Algorithm and Data</p>
              <p>A Correct Program Doing Harm</p>
              <p>Biased For What Use</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Two Sources</h2>
              <Code code={TwoSources} frame="code" lang="txt" title="IOC-1.D.1" showLineNumbers={false} />
              <p class="fragment">Say <b>both</b> in an answer. One of them is half the mark.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>The Program</h2>
              <Code code={Salary} frame="code" lang="javascript" title="A Starting Offer" showLineNumbers={false} />
              <p class="fragment">No syntax error. No logic error. It does exactly what it says.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Two Candidates, Same Experience</h2>
              <Code code={Amplify} frame="code" lang="txt" title="What It Produces" showLineNumbers={false} />
              <p class="fragment"><b class="text-sn-theme">The gap grew by ten percent.</b></p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Where Is the Bias?</h2>
              <p>Not in the arithmetic. The arithmetic is right.</p>
              <p class="fragment">
                <b>In the choice to use prior salary at all.</b>
              </p>
              <p class="fragment">
                If people were historically underpaid, prior salary <b>records that</b>
                &mdash; and this program treats the record as a fact about the person.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Nobody Wanted This</h2>
              <Code code={Intent} frame="code" lang="txt" title="Intentionally or Unintentionally" showLineNumbers={false} />
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>It Is Not a Bug</h2>
              <p>A bug is when a program does something other than what it was written to do.</p>
              <p class="fragment">This program does exactly what it was written to do.</p>
              <p class="fragment"><b>There is nothing to fix. There is something to decide.</b></p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Every Level</h2>
              <Code code={AllLevels} frame="code" lang="txt" title="IOC-1.D.3" showLineNumbers={false} />
              <p class="fragment">Bias is not one line. <b>It is a series of choices, most of them invisible later.</b></p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>The Question People Ask Wrong</h2>
              <Code code={Appropriate} frame="code" lang="txt" title="For What Use?" showLineNumbers={false} />
              <p class="fragment">A variable is not biased on its own. <b>A use of it can be.</b></p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>What You Are Asked to Do About It</h2>
              <p>
                <b>&quot;Programmers should take action to reduce bias in algorithms
                &hellip; as a way of combating existing human biases.&quot;</b>
              </p>
              <p class="fragment">Not <i>avoid creating</i> bias. <b>Combat existing bias.</b></p>
              <p class="fragment">The bias was already in the world. The program inherited it.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>One Thing That Helps</h2>
              <Code code={Collab} frame="code" lang="txt" title="Session 53, Again" showLineNumbers={false} />
              <p class="fragment">
                A room that has all been paid fairly will not think to check.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Today's Objectives</h2>
              <ul>
                <li>Naming both sources of bias in computing innovations</li>
                <li>Explaining how a correct program can produce a biased outcome</li>
                <li>Explaining that bias can be embedded at all levels of development</li>
                <li>Judging whether a variable is appropriate <b>for a given use</b></li>
                <li>Describing actions that reduce bias</li>
              </ul>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Key Terms</h2>
              <div class="text-start">
                <dl>
                  <dt>Computing bias</dt>
                  <dd>An innovation reflecting existing human bias, through its algorithms or through its data.</dd>
                  <dt>Proxy variable</dt>
                  <dd>A variable that stands in for something else &mdash; often for something you were not allowed to use. <b>This is our word, not the exam's</b> &mdash; the idea is examinable, the term is not.</dd>
                  <dt>Unintentional bias</dt>
                  <dd>Bias produced without anyone intending it. The exam says algorithms can be biased either way.</dd>
                </dl>
              </div>
            </section>
          </Presentation>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Build -->
        <section id="build">
          <h2>Today's Work</h2>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 1: Read the Program</h3>

          <Code code={Salary} frame="code" lang="javascript" title="offerFor" class="mt-3" />

          <p><b>Candidates: Rivera 52,000 / 5 years · Chen 41,000 / 5 years · Okafor 55,000 / 6 years · Sandoval 38,000 / 6 years.</b></p>

          <ol>
            <li><b>Work out all four offers by hand.</b></li>
            <li>Rivera and Chen have <b>identical experience</b>. What is the difference in their offers?</li>
            <li>What was the difference in their <b>prior</b> salaries?</li>
            <li><b>Compare those two numbers.</b> What has the algorithm done to the gap?</li>
            <li>Is there a syntax error? A logic error? <b>Is the arithmetic correct?</b></li>
          </ol>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 2: Locate the Bias</h3>

          <ol>
            <li>
              <b>Which line is the problem?</b> Quote it.
            </li>
            <li>
              <b>Explain in two sentences why that line can produce a biased outcome</b>,
              even though it contains no mistake.
            </li>
            <li>
              The exam names two sources of bias. <b>Which is this one</b>, and describe
              what the <b>other</b> kind would look like in this same program.
            </li>
            <li>
              <b>Is this bias intentional or unintentional?</b> Does the answer change
              whether it does harm?
            </li>
          </ol>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 3: Now Decide Something</h3>

          <p>
            <b>You are asked to fix it. There are three options and none of them is
            free.</b>
          </p>

          <table class="table table-sm align-middle mt-3">
            <thead><tr><th>Option</th><th>What it fixes</th><th>What it costs, or fails to fix</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td><b>A.</b> Remove <code>priorSalary</code>; base the offer on a fixed band for the role plus experience</td><td></td><td></td></tr>
              <tr><td><b>B.</b> Keep <code>priorSalary</code> but publish that it is used</td><td></td><td></td></tr>
              <tr><td><b>C.</b> Keep it, and add a rule that no offer may be more than 10% below the band midpoint</td><td></td><td></td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>

          <ol>
            <li><b>Which would you choose, and why?</b> Full sentences.</li>
            <li><b>What does your choice still not fix?</b> Every option leaves something.</li>
            <li>
              <b>Who should make this decision?</b> Not &quot;the programmer&quot; &mdash;
              say who, and why them.
            </li>
          </ol>

          <p>
            <b class="text-sn-theme">There is no correct answer to question 1 and there
            is a correct answer to question 2.</b> Every option leaves something behind,
            and naming it is the skill.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 4: Appropriate For What?</h3>

          <p>
            For each variable and use, say <b>appropriate</b> or <b>not</b>, and give a
            reason.
          </p>

          <table class="table table-sm align-middle mt-3">
            <thead><tr><th>Variable</th><th>Used to decide</th><th>Appropriate?</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>Age</td><td>a life insurance premium</td><td></td><td></td></tr>
              <tr><td>Age</td><td>who gets a job interview</td><td></td><td></td></tr>
              <tr><td>Postcode</td><td>a delivery time estimate</td><td></td><td></td></tr>
              <tr><td>Postcode</td><td>a loan application</td><td></td><td></td></tr>
              <tr><td>Typing speed</td><td>hiring a data entry clerk</td><td></td><td></td></tr>
              <tr><td>Which school you attended</td><td>hiring a graduate</td><td></td><td></td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>

          <p>
            <b>Two of these variables appear twice with different answers.</b> Explain
            what that proves about the question &quot;is this variable biased?&quot;
          </p>

          <p>
            <b>Then: the last row.</b> Which school somebody attended is not a protected
            characteristic and it may still be a <b>proxy</b> for one. Explain how.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Checkpoint -->
        <section id="checkpoint">
          <h2>Checkpoint</h2>

          <ul>
            <li>Name <b>both</b> sources of bias in computing innovations.</li>
            <li>Can a program with no errors in it be biased?</li>
            <li>At how many levels of development can bias be embedded?</li>
            <li>Why is &quot;is this variable biased?&quot; the wrong question?</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Reflection -->
        <section id="reflection">
          <h2>Reflection</h2>

          <p>Answer these before submitting your work.</p>

          <ol>
            <li>The program in Task 1 made an 11,000 gap into a 12,100 gap. Explain in one sentence why a percentage-based rule amplifies whatever difference it starts with.</li>
            <li>Session 44 said collecting more data does not remove bias. Explain why collecting more salary histories would not have helped this program either.</li>
            <li>The exam says programmers should act to <b>combat existing</b> human biases, not merely avoid adding new ones. Explain what that asks of a programmer beyond &quot;be fair.&quot;</li>
          </ol>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Submit -->
        <section id="submit">
          <h2>Submit</h2>

          <p>All four tasks, plus your reflection.</p>

          <h1
            class="text-center mt-5 text-sn-theme border border-primary slow-flash"
          >
            Activity Complete
          </h1>
        </section>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
